{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who governed the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He inherited his title from his father Raymond II who died by assassination. The young Raymond faced many dangers because his rule began under the regency of his mother Hodierna. She managed the affairs of the state until he reached adulthood. Raymond later married Eschiva of Bures and became the lord of Galilee through this union. His political career involved complex alliances with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire. He often clashed with other nobles over influence and territory within the Latin East.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born around the year 1140 and became the count after his father fell victim to the Hashashin sect. The Assassins killed Raymond II in 1152 while the boy was still a minor. His mother Hodierna took charge of the government and protected him until he came of age. This period was crucial for establishing his future authority over the northern crusader state. He eventually assumed full control of Tripoli and began to expand his power base.\n\n*Relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nThe relationship between Raymond and the kings of Jerusalem was often tense and difficult. He supported Baldwin IV despite the king's leprosy and served as regent during the monarchs youth. Raymond opposed the faction led by Guy of Lusignan who married Sibylla. This conflict created deep divisions within the Christian leadership before the Battle of Hattin. Raymond tried to prevent a disastrous war against Saladin but his warnings were ignored by the more aggressive nobles.\n\n*Final Years and Legacy*\nRaymond died in 1187 shortly after the catastrophic defeat at Hattin. He passed away in Tripoli while suffering from an illness that may have been related to stress. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli 